Sure 👍 Here is the **clear differentiation of Set, Multi-Set, and Unordered Set in C++, Java, and Python**.

# Set vs Multi-Set vs Unordered Set

| Feature              | **Set**                   | **Multi-Set**          | **Unordered Set**    |
| -------------------- | ------------------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------- |
| Duplicates           | ❌ No                      | ✅ Yes                  | ❌ No                 |
| Sorted order         | ✅ Yes                     | ✅ Yes                  | ❌ No                 |
| Stores frequency     | ❌ No                      | ✅ Multiple occurrences | ❌ No                 |
| Main purpose         | Unique + sorted           | Duplicate + sorted     | Unique + fast lookup |
| C++                  | `set`                     | `multiset`             | `unordered_set`      |
| Java                 | `TreeSet`                 | `TreeMap` + count*     | `HashSet`            |
| Python               | `set` + sorting if needed | `Counter`              | `set`                |
| Average insert       | O(log n)                  | O(log n)               | O(1)                 |
| Average find         | O(log n)                  | O(log n)               | O(1)                 |
| Average erase/remove | O(log n)                  | O(log n)               | O(1)                 |

* Java standard library doesn't have a direct `Multiset`; `TreeMap<E,Integer>` is a common implementation.

