# C++ Comparison

| Operation          | `set`           | `multiset`         | `unordered_set` |
| ------------------ | --------------- | ------------------ | --------------- |
| Duplicate          | ❌               | ✅                  | ❌               |
| Sorted             | ✅               | ✅                  | ❌               |
| Insert             | `insert()`      | `insert()`         | `insert()`      |
| Find               | `find()`        | `find()`           | `find()`        |
| Remove             | `erase()`       | `erase()`          | `erase()`       |
| Count              | `count()` → 0/1 | `count()` → 0 to n | `count()` → 0/1 |
| Average complexity | O(log n)        | O(log n)           | O(1)            |
| Worst-case         | O(log n)        | O(log n)           | O(n)            |

### Important C++ point

For:

```cpp
multiset<int> ms = {2, 2, 5, 5, 5};
```

```cpp
ms.count(5);
```

returns:

```text
3
```

But for:

```cpp
set<int> s = {2, 5};
```

```cpp
s.count(5);
```

returns only:

```text
1
```

because duplicates aren't allowed.

